capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332731" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea"} {"question" "q-332731" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332731" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2xe^{6x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while ${e}^{2x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-3x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-3x}-3x{e}^{-3x})+2\\beta {e}^{2x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha $ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-3x){e}^{-3x}+2\\beta {e}^{2x})+3(\\alpha x{e}^{-3x}+\\beta {e}^{2x}) & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1-3x+3x)\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+(2\\beta +3\\beta ){e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-3x}+5\\beta {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{-3x}+10{e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of ${e}^{-3x}$ and ${e}^{2x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha =6\\,\\text{and}\\,5\\beta =10,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha =6\\,\\text{and}\\,\\beta =2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=6x{e}^{-3x}+2{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332795" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea"} {"question" "q-332795" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332795" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6xe^{-3x}+2e^{2x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x}$, find the particular solution to the equation.\n\n${y}_{p}(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{3x},$ and ${e}^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{3x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{3x}+3x{e}^{3x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+3x){e}^{3x})-3\\alpha x{e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ (1+3x-3x)\\alpha {e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{3x} & =8{e}^{3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332730" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea"} {"question" "q-332730" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332730" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "8xe^{3x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-333179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=8{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{3x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-333179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{5x},$ and ${e}^{5x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant that is to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′}=\\alpha {e}^{5x}\\cdot 5=5\\alpha {e}^{5x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5\\alpha {e}^{5x}-3\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ 2\\alpha {e}^{5x} & =8{e}^{5x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4{e}^{5x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+4{e}^{5x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-333179" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea"} {"question" "q-333179" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-333179" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Ae^{3x}+4e^{5x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-2y=5{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{2x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{2x}+2x{e}^{2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+2x){e}^{2x})-2\\alpha x{e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ (1+2x-2x)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{2x} & =5{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{2x}+5x{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332895" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea"} {"question" "q-332895" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/diffe