 \\alpha  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=4x{e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}+4x{e}^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332898" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea"} {"question" "q-332898" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332898" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Ae^{3x}+4xe^{3x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-333168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-7y=5{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{7x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-333168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-7{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{7x},$ and ${e}^{7x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{7x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{7x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{7x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{7x}+7x{e}^{7x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1+7x){e}^{7x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1+7x){e}^{7x})-7\\alpha x{e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ (1+7x-7x)\\alpha {e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{7x} & =5{e}^{7x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{7x}+5x{e}^{7x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-333168" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea"} {"question" "q-333168" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-333168" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "Ae^{7x}+5xe^{7x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=5{e}^{-x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-4y=5{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $5{e}^{-x},$ and ${e}^{-x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-\\alpha {e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(-\\alpha {e}^{-x})-4(\\alpha {e}^{-x}) & =5{e}^{-x} \\\\ (-1-4)\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =5{e}^{-x} \\\\ -5\\alpha {e}^{-x} & =5{e}^{-x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-1 & =-1 \\\\ A & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332905" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea"} {"question" "q-332905" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332905" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-e^{-x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=7{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=0\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-3y=7{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $7{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-2\\alpha {e}^{-2x}-3\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =7{e}^{-2x} \\\\ -5\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =7{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{3x}-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=0$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-\\frac{7}{5} & =0 \\\\ A & =\\frac{7}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{3x}-\\frac{7}{5}{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332906" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea"} {"question" "q-332906" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332906" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{7}{5}e^{3x}-\\frac{7}{5}e^{-2x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=6{e}^{2x},\\,y(0)=4\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}-5y=6{e}^{2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $6{e}^{2x},$ and ${e}^{2x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=2\\alpha {e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(2\\alpha {e}^{2x})-5(\\alpha {e}^{2x}) & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ (2-5)\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ -3\\alpha {e}^{2x} & =6{e}^{2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{5x}-2{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=4$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2 & =4 \\\\ A & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=6{e}^{5x}-2{e}^{2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332903" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea"} {"question" "q-332903" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332903" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "6e^{5x}-2e^{2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=-5{e}^{-3x},\\,y(0)=8\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=-5{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $-5{e}^{-3x},$ and ${e}^{-3x}$ is not a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha {e}^{-3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}^{′}=-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-3\\alpha {e}^{-3x}+2\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =-5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{-3x} & =-5{e}^{-3x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=8$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+5 & =8 \\\\ A & =3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=3{e}^{-2x}+5{e}^{-3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332909" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea"} {"question" "q-332909" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332909" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "3e^{-2x}+5e^{-3x}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x},\\,y(0)=-1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+2y=8{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+2{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-2x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $8{e}^{-2x},$ and ${e}^{-2x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}=\\alpha x{e}^{-2x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nWe calculate the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ using the product rule for differentiation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-2x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-2x}-2x{e}^{-2x}) \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\alpha ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-2x){e}^{-2x})+2(\\alpha x{e}^{-2x}) & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ (1-2x+2x)\\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-2x} & =8{e}^{-2x} \\\\ \\alpha  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$\n\nWe can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=-1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A+0 & =-1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-2x}+8x{e}^{-2x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332936" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "3608d4e1729d52dbbd5e37178cdaa0e8914aebbc7a9b4520adf64c212f158fea"} {"question" "q-332936" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "q-332936" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-e^{-2x}+8xe^{-2x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/state.json" "file_sha256" "1e201f73a2dcba8817f27f103d4ea9dbb053badeeb1ccdea5bbebfb7a0ae388b"} {"question" "e-21615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\n\\frac {\\textrm {d} y} {\\textrm {d} x} - 2 y =  e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x}\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  A e ^ {2x}$, find the particular solution $y_p(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/example-21615.json" "file_sha256" "b26c3aec81b7d61b80567135133560bd6620429883615045f8b1efb796e5b9ca"} {"question" "e-21615" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $e ^ {x} - 2 e^{3x},$ and neither $e^{x}$ nor $e^{3x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, so we assume a particular solution of the form\n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha e^{x} + \\beta e^{3x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\textrm{d}y_p}{\\textrm{d}x} = \\alpha e^{x} + 3\\beta e^{3x}.\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha {e}^{x}+3\\beta {e}^{3x})-2(\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x}) & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ (\\alpha -2\\alpha ){e}^{x}+(3\\beta -2\\beta ){e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x} \\\\ -\\alpha {e}^{x}+\\beta {e}^{3x} & ={e}^{x}-2{e}^{3x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{x}$ and $e^{3x},$ respectively, gives\n\n$$\n-\\alpha = 1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = -1, \\quad \\quad \\beta = -2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = - e ^{x} - 2 e^{3x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/example-21615.json" "file_sha256" "b26c3aec81b7d61b80567135133560bd6620429883615045f8b1efb796e5b9ca"} {"question" "e-21621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that the differential equation \n\n$$\ny' + 5y = 15e^{-5x} + 12e^{x},\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution $y_c(x) =  Ae^{-5x}$, find the particular solution to the equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/example-21621.json" "file_sha256" "ac147dec3b472c4e202a93344a3f3b9168d5739c310a5f9e8b74577e993f5074"} {"question" "e-21621" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation with constant coefficients on the left-hand side, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nWe see that the right-hand side is $15 e^{-5x} + 12 e^{x},$ and $e^{-5x}$ is a term in the complementary solution, while $e^{x}$ is not, so we assume that a particular solution takes the form \n\n$$\ny_p = \\alpha x  e ^ {-5 x} + \\beta e^{x},\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ are constants that are to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of $y_p$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p}^{′} & =(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha (x{e}^{-5x})^{′}+\\beta ({e}^{x})^{′} \\\\  & =\\alpha ({e}^{-5x}-5x{e}^{-5x})+\\beta {e}^{x} \\\\  & =\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha$ and $\\beta,$ we substitute $y_p$ and $y_p'$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(\\alpha (1-5x){e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x})+5(\\alpha x{e}^{-5x}+\\beta {e}^{x}) & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ (1-5x+5x)\\alpha {e}^{-5x}+(\\beta +5\\beta ){e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x} \\\\ \\alpha {e}^{-5x}+6\\beta {e}^{x} & =15{e}^{-5x}+12{e}^{x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEquating coefficients of $e^{-5x}$ and $e^{x}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad 6\\beta = 12,\n$$\n\nso, we have\n\n$$\n\\alpha = 15 \\quad \\text{and} \\quad \\beta = 2.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution $y_p(x)$ is\n\n$$\ny_p(x) = 15x  e ^ {-5x} + 2 e^{x}.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14039029/example-21621.json" "file_sha256" "a